---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PostInstall from '../../components/PostInstall.astro';
import InstallNudge from '../../components/InstallNudge.astro';
import { groups } from '../../data/checklist.es.js';
import lockupPrint from '../../components/brand-lockup-print.svg?raw';
import printQr from '../../components/print-qr.svg?raw';
/*
 * THE PRE-GIG CHECKLIST, NEUTRAL LATIN AMERICAN SPANISH.
 *
 * Source of truth for structure, classes, ids, data attributes, localStorage
 * keys, CSS and behaviour: src/pages/checklist.astro. ONLY the human readable
 * copy, the internal hrefs and the language of the printed sheet differ. If
 * the English page changes, change this one in the same commit.
 *
 * The 48 item labels and the 7 group titles live in src/data/checklist.es.js,
 * whose keys, ids and levels are identical to the English catalogue on
 * purpose: they are the localStorage identity of a ticked item.
 *
 * `path` stays the ENGLISH path. Base.astro derives the /es URL, the
 * canonical and the hreflang from it.
 */

/**
 * Interactive pre-gig checklist. Prevention side, outside the tunnel.
 * Tick boxes with a live readiness meter; state persists per device.
 *   - localStorage SMG_CHECKLIST_V1   = { itemKey: true }        (ticks)
 *   - localStorage SMG_CHECKLIST_MODE = 'basic' | 'advanced'     (depth)
 *   - localStorage SMG_CHECKLIST_OPEN = { groupId: false }       (collapsed)
 * Basic/Advanced toggle keeps the default list short on mobile; Advanced
 * reveals the full kit. Each category collapses independently via the arrow.
 * Readiness counts only the items visible in the current mode.
 */

/**
 * THE REDESIGN (Antonio's spec, 2026-07-27, agreed in full exchange):
 * two FIXED professional standards plus total freedom in Custom.
 *  - Basic answers "can I leave home right now and complete this gig?"
 *    and includes the whole working day: music, gear, pockets, logistics.
 *  - Pro (his "Advanced") answers "can I handle the unexpected?" and adds
 *    backups, technical kit, recovery, travel and comfort.
 *  - NO conditional items, ever: every DJ sees the same list and the same
 *    total, so 19/19 means the same thing in every booth on earth. Smart
 *    per-setup logic was considered and killed by Antonio on purpose
 *    ("the integrity of the progress system"). Custom is where workflows
 *    differ: it starts as a copy of Pro and becomes entirely the DJ's own,
 *    including their OWN SECTIONS.
 * Item keys survive from the old list wherever the item survived, so
 * existing ticks carry over. Icons are monochrome stroke SVGs (standing
 * rule: never emoji in headers).
 */

const total = groups.reduce((n, g) => n + g.items.length, 0);
---
<Base
  title="El Checklist Pre-Gig para DJs | Save My Gig"
  description="Checklist pre-gig para marcar antes de salir de casa: música, equipo, kit personal y logística. Guardado en tu dispositivo y listo para imprimir."
  path="/checklist"
  lang="es"
>
  <main class="wrap" id="top">

    <!-- Status tag, desktop only (Antonio): every hub page carries one
         (Emergency_Mode, Prevention_Hub...) and the checklist was the odd
         one out after "Prevention_Hub" was removed here for being the wrong
         label. Its own honest tag now; hidden on phones, where he does not
         want the extra line above the H1. -->
    <div class="status-line green sl-desktop">Checklist_Pre_Gig</div>
    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1rem">SAL DE CASA <span class="accent">LISTO</span></h1>
    <!-- Desktop breaks before the storage sentence (Antonio: statically
         cleaner); phones wrap naturally. Returning visitors on a phone have
         read this already, so it auto-hides for them and gives the space
         back, no dismiss chrome needed. -->
    {/* COPY PASS 2026-08-03, REVERTED THE SAME DAY. A rewrite replaced the
        second half with "What you forget at home, you cannot fix in the
        booth". Antonio: "I think this was easier to understand", meaning
        the original. He is right on two counts and this line is now
        PROTECTED, so do not "improve" it again:
        1. The rewrite used an INVERSION, which is a rhetorical structure
           rather than plain speech. It reads punchier and parses slower, and
           this page is opened by someone packing to leave.
        2. This copy is going to Spanish and Portuguese. Inversions translate
           stilted; plain declarative sentences survive translation intact.
        Same reasoning protects "registration loads it on any other", which
        the rewrite split into two sentences to be technically precise about
        registration versus account. Nobody misreads it, and one flowing
        sentence beats two correct ones here. */}
    <p class="dim page-intro" id="pageIntro" style="margin-bottom:1.6rem">Marca todo antes de salir, para que el gig nunca empiece con algo faltando. <span class="intro-b">Tu lista se guarda en este dispositivo, y el registro la carga en cualquier otro.</span></p>
    <script is:inline>
      (function () {
        try {
          if (window.matchMedia('(max-width: 640px)').matches &&
              (localStorage.getItem('SMG_CHECKLIST_MODE') || localStorage.getItem('SMG_CHECKLIST_V1'))) {
            document.getElementById('pageIntro').style.display = 'none';
          }
        } catch (e) { /* storage blocked: keep the intro */ }
      })();
    </script>

    {/* LOS DOS ENLACES DE CONTEXTO SE MUDARON AL CARD DE INSTALACION AL PIE DE
        ESTA PAGINA (2026-08-06, pedido de Antonio: el tope tiene que quedar
        limpio). No se perdio nada: /install siempre fue el tema de ese card, y
        /prepare quedo ahi como enlace secundario discreto. Razon completa en
        src/pages/checklist.astro. El parrafo de arriba sigue siendo copy
        PROTEGIDA y nada se doblo de vuelta dentro de el. */}

    <!-- Round 11.5 (Antonio, "very very very important"): a standing
         reminder that this runs on servers, and servers can and do go down
         -- his own example the same day, an hour of Claude itself being
         unreachable. The full legal language (no warranty, DJ's own
         responsibility to back up, planned-maintenance window) lives at
         /legal/disclaimer; this is the short version sitting right above
         the very buttons (Download list, Email list) that make the backup
         happen. UPDATED same round, Antonio's own follow-up: "this should
         be one time only... they click it, they close it, and it will
         never appear again unless they clean the cookies or the cache" --
         dismiss-forever via localStorage, same mechanism the old edit-hint
         box (round 9) and the mode-hint box (round 3) used before him.
         Starts `hidden` in markup and is only unhidden by the inline
         script below if not already dismissed, so a returning dismisser
         never sees a flash of it before JS runs. -->
    <div class="backup-note" id="backupNote" role="note" hidden>
      <p class="bn-t">
        <!-- ROUND 11.5 FOLLOW-UP 2: reworded after tester Maya read the
             original ("technology can fail, including our servers") as
             hardcore/distrustful. Antonio proposed a friendlier version
             built around "plan b and plan c" / "triple backed up"; this is
             that idea tightened into house style. Same underlying message
             (no uptime guarantee, backup is on the DJ), same link to the
             permanent, non-dismissible legal language at /legal/disclaimer
             -- only the ON-PAGE reminder's tone changed. -->
        {/* COPY PASS 2026-08-03. The closing line was "That's triple backed
            up", which counted the copies without saying why the third one
            matters, and is not a phrase anyone says out loud. "Paper still
            works when the phone is dead" gives the reason, and it is the one
            argument that actually makes a DJ print something. 34 words
            against the previous 37, so it is shorter and does more.
            His plan B / plan C framing is kept: it is his, and it is what
            made this notice read as advice instead of a disclaimer. */}
        <strong class="bn-h">Consejo serio.</strong>
        Hasta la mejor tecnología falla, la nuestra incluida. Ten un plan B y un plan C: descarga tu lista e imprímela. El papel sigue funcionando cuando el celular ya no tiene batería. Mira el <a href="/es/legal/disclaimer">aviso legal</a>.
      </p>
      <button type="button" class="bn-close" id="bnClose" aria-label="Descartar este aviso">&times;</button>
    </div>
    <script is:inline>
      (function () {
        var KEY = 'SMG_BACKUP_NOTE_DISMISSED';
        var box = document.getElementById('backupNote');
        if (!box) return;
        try {
          if (localStorage.getItem(KEY) === '1') return; // stays hidden, dismissed before
        } catch (e) { /* storage blocked: show it, the safer default */ }
        box.hidden = false;
        var closeBtn = document.getElementById('bnClose');
        if (closeBtn) closeBtn.addEventListener('click', function () {
          box.hidden = true;
          try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, '1'); } catch (e) { /* blocked: won't persist, harmless */ }
        });
      })();
    </script>

    <!-- Mode switch + the account/sync chip share one row (Antonio's ruling,
         2026-07-29 polish round: the chip lives beside Custom, not as a
         full-width row under the meter). Signed out it is a dim verb, Sync ,
         tap it, it opens the box and asks you to sign in. Signed in it is a
         green state, Synced, because the sync already happened; tapping it
         just re-opens the same box to sign out, delete, or see the email. -->
    <div class="mode-row">
      <!-- Vertical dividers between the three segments (Antonio, round 4:
           this is where he actually wanted the hairline, not on the Sync
           chip, moved here). Same recipe as everywhere else it's used:
           align-self: stretch so it always matches the row's own height,
           colored to var(--edge). -->
      <div class="mode-switch" role="group" aria-label="Profundidad del checklist">
        <button type="button" class="mode-btn is-on" data-mode-set="basic" aria-pressed="true">Base</button>
        <span class="mode-switch-div" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <!-- LOCKED IS VISIBLE BEFORE THE TAP (2026-08-06). Tapping Advanced or
             Custom while signed out appeared to do nothing: the segmented
             control did not move (correctly, the mode did not change) and the
             account card opened around 140px further down the page with no
             visual link to the tap at all. A calm visitor reads that as a
             broken button, not as a gate.
             The lock ships in the MARKUP, not applied by script on load, for
             the same reason the groups ship collapsed (2026-08-04, CLS 0.349
             -> 0.115): a first-time visitor IS locked, so the initial HTML
             should say so rather than repainting once a 58KB module has
             parsed. unlockAll() below strips both the class and the .sr-only
             word the moment this device is registered.
             13px, currentColor, stroke 2.2, so it inherits the chip's own
             colour and stays legible at booth brightness instead of being a
             dimmed-out state nobody can see in the dark. -->
        <button type="button" class="mode-btn is-locked" data-mode-set="advanced" aria-pressed="false">Avanzado<svg class="mode-lock" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="13" height="13" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="11" width="16" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v4"/></svg><span class="sr-only mode-lock-sr">Bloqueado, necesita una cuenta gratis.</span></button>
        <span class="mode-switch-div" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button type="button" class="mode-btn is-locked" data-mode-set="custom" aria-pressed="false">Personal<svg class="mode-lock" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="13" height="13" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="11" width="16" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v4"/></svg><span class="sr-only mode-lock-sr">Bloqueado, necesita una cuenta gratis.</span></button>
      </div>
      <div class="mode-row-right">
        <!-- Info toggle (Antonio, 2026-07-30 round 3): now shown on BOTH
             touch and desktop, but they do different jobs. On touch, off/gray
             by default; tap turns it on/amber, shows the instruction once,
             and while on, tapping any of the six covered controls shows that
             control's info instead of using it. On desktop hover-a-beat
             already always works regardless of this button, so clicking it
             here is purely a discovery aid: on/amber just opens a static
             reminder that hovering any control for a second explains it, off
             again closes it. Desktop never gates real clicks behind this. -->
        <button type="button" class="explain-btn" id="explainInfoBtn" aria-label="Mostrar las instrucciones de los botones" aria-pressed="false">ⓘ</button>
        <!-- Rectangle to match the toolbar buttons (Antonio: the pill "looked
             strange"). Round 4: the internal divider Antonio asked for turned
             out to belong on the mode-switch above, not here, removed from
             this button, back to plain text + chevron. Chevron uses the SAME
             convention as the category boxes (Music, DJ Gear): right when
             closed, down when open, not the Lists header's up/down pair
             (Antonio's ask, round 3: match Music, not Lists). -->
        <button type="button" class="acct-chip" id="acctLine" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="acctCard">
          <span class="acct-chip-state" id="acctChipText">Sync</span>
          <span class="ls-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- One gate, two doors. This panel now also serves the PDF download, so
         the title and first line are set from JS depending on what the DJ
         asked for. A second unlock panel with its own copy would have drifted
         from this one within a week. -->
    <div class="unlock" id="unlock" hidden>
      <!-- Antonio's framing, and it is better than what was here. This is NOT
           "your email is the price". That reads like a transaction and it makes
           a professional feel charged for something we call free. It is
           "free for registered users", which is how every real tool works and
           how a working DJ expects to be treated.
           Round 5 (Antonio): the panel used to show the headline, the full
           note, three fields and two sign-in paths all at once, "too much
           information". Title cut to four words; Google is now the default
           single action and the whole email form hides behind one toggle,
           so the box shows one thing at a time instead of everything at
           once. -->
      <p class="unlock-title" id="unlockTitle">Desbloquea Avanzado y Personal</p>
      <!-- Round 5, second pass (Antonio, live-testing right after the first
           round-5 push): "access is instant, no password... you don't need
           to say that", cut entirely. Element stays in the DOM (id kept)
           because openGate() still swaps its text for the PDF/email-list
           gate variants; hidden via CSS instead of removed from markup, so
           JS doesn't need a guard added everywhere it touches unlockCopy. -->
      <p class="dim unlock-copy" id="unlockCopy" hidden>Acceso al instante, sin contraseña. El botón de correo lleva tu lista a cualquier otro dispositivo.</p>

      <!-- Google first now (Antonio, round 5): most DJs tap this and never
           see the fields below it. Antonio's OLD ruling required the artist
           name before Google would even respond (a transparent blocker sat
           over the iframe) so the homepage greeting always had a name to
           use; that blocker is gone now that the field it was guarding is
           hidden by default too, which would have left Google permanently
           blocked with no visible way to unblock it. The name still gets
           collected, just after sign-in instead of before: the Sync card's
           "so we know what to call you" prompt (#acctProfile, further down
           this file) already exists for exactly this and now covers the
           gate's Google path too, same as it already covers the card's.
           Flagging this because it reopens a documented ruling; easy to
           revert if that trade-off is wrong. -->
      <div class="unlock-alt" id="unlockAlt" hidden>
        <div class="gsi-wrap" id="gsiGateWrap">
          <div class="gsi-holder" id="gsiGateBtn"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Only revealed once Google actually renders (JS below): with no
           Google configured there is no alternative to offer, so the form
           stays the default, visible-on-load path instead (progressive
           enhancement, works with JS off too). -->
      <button type="button" class="unlock-email-toggle" id="unlockEmailToggle" hidden>¿Sin Google? Sin problema. Usa tu otro correo.</button>

      <!-- One required field, two optional ones. Antonio wanted name, surname,
           artist name and Instagram, all required, so he can follow every DJ
           who registers. The follow-back idea is genuinely good and stays. The
           required part does not: every mandatory field cuts completion, and a
           DJ in a hurry abandons forms that feel like paperwork. So email is
           the only requirement, and the Instagram field carries the promise
           that makes people WANT to fill it. Optional fields that offer
           something convert; required fields that demand something do not. -->
      <form class="unlock-form" id="unlockForm" novalidate>
        <input type="email" name="email" autocomplete="email" inputmode="email" placeholder="tu@email.com" aria-label="Tu mejor correo" aria-describedby="unlockMsg" required />
        <!-- REQUIRED since 2026-07-27 (Antonio: the personal touch is worth
             the field, and everyone has a first name, techs included). The
             homepage greets by this name where it fits. Trade-off noted at
             the time: a second required field costs some conversions. Stays
             required on the email path specifically: unlike Google, this
             path never opens a session, so there is no later screen to
             collect it in. -->
        <input type="text" name="artist" required autocomplete="nickname" placeholder="Obligatorio: nombre artístico o nombre de pila" aria-label="Obligatorio: nombre artístico o nombre de pila" aria-describedby="unlockMsg" maxlength="80" />
        <!-- The follow promise lives INSIDE the field now (Antonio): one line
             less under the form, and the pitch sits exactly where the answer
             goes. Long placeholder ellipsizes on narrow phones; the handle
             hint is front-loaded so the cut only ever costs the tail. -->
        <!-- Reads in cause-and-effect order (Antonio): the handle, then why
             we want it, then that it is optional. -->
        <input type="text" name="instagram" autocomplete="off" inputmode="text" placeholder="Opcional: @tu.instagram.artistico, seguimos tu trabajo con gusto" aria-label="Opcional: tu Instagram artístico, seguimos tu trabajo con gusto" aria-describedby="unlockMsg" maxlength="80" />
        <!-- Register + the anti-spam promise share a row on desktop (Antonio:
             everything moves up a little); they stack again on phones. -->
        <div class="unlock-actions">
          <!-- .btn-quiet-register, not .btn-quiet-danger (round 4 split):
               Register is a signup action, not an emergency one, and keeps
               its original transparent-until-hover look now that
               .btn-quiet-danger got a light-red idle fill for the actual
               "Start Emergency Mode" buttons sitewide. -->
          <button type="submit" class="btn btn-quiet-register">Registrarme</button>
          <!-- Antonio's anti-spam promise, trimmed again round 5: the ad-copy
             framing ("site news, new tools, a product that helps your
             career") was pulling eyes away from Register. Still honest,
             still links Privacy, just one line now. -->
          <p class="unlock-note">Correos de vez en cuando, te das de baja cuando quieras. <a href="/es/legal/privacy">Privacidad</a></p>
        </div>
      </form>
      <p class="unlock-msg" id="unlockMsg" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </div>

    <section class="readiness" id="readiness" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="readiness-top">
        <span class="readiness-label">Preparación</span>
        <span class="readiness-pct" id="pct">0%</span>
      </div>
      <div class="meter"><div class="meter-fill" id="meterFill"></div></div>
      <p class="readiness-sub" id="sub">0 listos</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ACCOUNT / SYNC (2026-07-29 build, Antonio's rulings; chip relocated
         to the mode row in the same day's polish pass). The card opens from
         the Sync/Synced chip up by the mode switch, but the card itself
         lives here in the DOM, right under the readiness meter, which is
         where it reads best once open. Registration and its gate are
         untouched; the account is the optional layer on top. No passwords
         exist anywhere: Google, or an emailed one-tap link. -->
    <div class="acct-card" id="acctCard" hidden>
      <!-- THE CARD NAMES WHAT WAS ASKED FOR (2026-08-06). Opened from the
           Sync chip this card is self-explanatory; opened by tapping a locked
           mode it was an unlabelled account box a screen away from the tap,
           and the visitor had to infer the connection. Filled by JS only on
           the locked-tab path, so the Sync path is byte for byte what it was.
           A <p>, not a heading element: .unlock-title is the same thing on the
           gate panel below and this borrows its exact styling, and inserting a
           real h3 here would sit above the seven category h2s and break the
           document outline this page fixed on 2026-08-04. -->
      <p class="unlock-title acct-gate-h" id="acctGateH" hidden></p>
      <div id="acctOut">
        <!-- Round 7 (Antonio): this is now the FIRST thing shown for
             Advanced/Custom too, not just Sync, so the wording has to work
             as a cold open, not just a returning-user reminder. -->
        <p class="dim acct-copy">Una cuenta, sin contraseña, nunca. Tu lista queda guardada en tu cuenta y te sigue a cualquier dispositivo donde entres.</p>
        <!-- Google renders its own button in here when the client id is
             configured; until then the holder is empty and email carries
             the whole flow. -->
        <div class="gsi-holder" id="gsiBtn"></div>
        <!-- Round 5, fourth pass (Antonio): the old "or, sign in by email:"
             divider still read as two required steps stacked together, not
             a clear either/or, "we must clarify for the user that is not
             very intelligent." Replaced with the SAME pattern the gate uses
             now: Google alone by default, a toggle link reveals the email
             form. JS below hides #acctForm and shows this toggle the moment
             Google actually renders (mirrors the gate's #unlockEmailToggle
             logic exactly); with no Google configured this never runs, so
             the form just stays visible, the only path there is. -->
        <button type="button" class="unlock-email-toggle" id="acctEmailToggle" hidden>¿Sin Google? Sin problema. Usa tu otro correo.</button>
        <form class="acct-form" id="acctForm" novalidate>
          <input type="email" name="email" autocomplete="email" inputmode="email" placeholder="tu@email.com" aria-label="Tu correo" aria-describedby="acctMsg" />
          <button type="submit" class="btn btn-neutral">Mándame un enlace de acceso</button>
        </form>
        <p class="acct-msg" id="acctMsg" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
      </div>
      <div id="acctIn" hidden>
        <!-- The email lives here now that the chip up top just says Synced
             (Antonio: the chip is a state, not a place to read the address). -->
        <p class="dim acct-copy">Sincronizado con <span class="acct-who" id="acctEmail"></span></p>
        <!-- Round 10 (Antonio): the "Tap Sync to close this." line this
             replaced no longer earns its space -- clicking/tapping ANYWHERE
             outside this card now closes it too (see the document-level
             capture listener below), which is the same familiar
             click-away-to-dismiss pattern every dropdown/popover already
             uses, so it needs no instructions at all. -->
        <!-- First-sign-in completion (Antonio's ruling: the account asks
             what the gate asks, same fields, same promise). Shows until
             the account has an artist name; prefilled when this device
             already knows it; never blocks sync. -->
        <form class="acct-form acct-profile" id="acctProfile" hidden novalidate>
          <p class="dim acct-copy" style="flex:1 1 100%;margin-bottom:0.1rem">Para saber cómo llamarte:</p>
          <input type="text" name="artist" required autocomplete="nickname" placeholder="Obligatorio: nombre artístico o nombre de pila" aria-label="Obligatorio: nombre artístico o nombre de pila" aria-describedby="acctProfileMsg" maxlength="80" />
          <input type="text" name="instagram" autocomplete="off" inputmode="text" placeholder="Opcional: @tu.instagram.artistico, seguimos tu trabajo con gusto" aria-label="Opcional: tu Instagram artístico, seguimos tu trabajo con gusto" aria-describedby="acctProfileMsg" maxlength="80" />
          <button type="submit" class="btn btn-neutral">Guardar</button>
          <p class="acct-msg" id="acctProfileMsg" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </form>
        <div class="acct-actions">
          <!-- Round 7 (Antonio): "signing out by accident" is a real risk on
               a single tap, so this gets the same two-tap arm as Delete
               account and Reset -- same stacked-cell trick, width can never
               change, innerHTML swaps stay banned. -->
          <button type="button" class="btn btn-neutral acct-signout" id="signOutBtn">
            <span class="rs-idle">Cerrar sesión</span>
            <span class="rs-arm" aria-hidden="true">¿Seguro?</span>
          </button>
          <!-- Same stacked-cell armed pattern as Reset list: width can
               never change, innerHTML swaps stay banned. -->
          <button type="button" class="btn btn-neutral acct-del" id="delAcctBtn">
            <span class="rs-idle">Borrar cuenta</span>
            <span class="rs-arm" aria-hidden="true">Clic de nuevo</span>
          </button>
        </div>
        <!-- Round 5, third pass (Antonio): this used to always show "Deletes
             your account..." even at rest, which read as a warning for
             something the DJ hadn't done yet. Empty/hidden until Delete
             account is actually armed; JS below fills it in only then.
             Round 7: re-centered under the box (it was reading left-aligned
             against the centered buttons above it) and reworded shorter,
             dropping the "this session stays on the device" clause. -->
        <p class="dim acct-fine err" id="acctDelFine" hidden></p>
        <p class="acct-msg" id="acctMsg2" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Named lists (Custom mode + signed in only, Antonio's rulings 4, 5
         and his mobile review 2026-07-29): ONE collapsed row that names the
         active list; tap the arrow, the pills unfold, pick, it folds back.
         The collapse doctrine applied to lists ("harmonica"), identical on
         phone and desktop because one behavior is simpler than two. Pills
         switch, the field creates; the Delete toggle below reveals each
         pill's x, which arms then deletes, same two-tap contract as every
         destructive action here. -->
    <div class="list-switch" id="listSwitch" hidden>
      <button type="button" class="ls-head" id="lsHead" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="lsBody">
        <span class="ls-label">Listas</span>
        <span class="ls-active" id="lsActiveName"></span>
        <span class="ls-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </button>
      <div class="ls-body" id="lsBody" hidden>
        <div class="ls-pills" id="lsPills"></div>
        <!-- Round 11.5 (Antonio, live review): the old two-button row ("+
             New" text button, "Delete" text button that ALSO revealed the
             reorder handle -- his own complaint: "why is the delete button
             giving me the option to move?") is now three separate, single-
             purpose icon+label buttons. Icon always shows; the word label
             hides on touch (@media hover:none below) so mobile gets a
             compact icon row instead of three full text buttons eating the
             line, same "icon on phone, icon+word on desktop" split already
             used elsewhere on this page. Move icon is the EXACT SAME sideways
             drag-handle SVG as the per-pill handle it now gates, reused
             rather than invented, per Antonio's explicit instruction. -->
        <div class="ls-toolbar" id="lsToolbar">
          <button type="button" class="ls-tool-btn ls-new-btn" id="lsNewBtn" aria-label="Lista nueva">
            <span class="ls-btn-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M8 2v12M2 8h12"/></svg></span>
            <span class="ls-btn-label">Nueva</span>
          </button>
          <!-- Round 11.5 follow-up (Antonio): labeled "Move" originally, but
               this toggle also reveals the rename pencil now, not just the
               drag handle -- "it cannot just be Move." "Edit" was ruled out
               deliberately: EDIT LIST already exists lower on this page for
               a different thing (notes/sections/items), and reusing the
               word here would read as the same control. "Manage" covers
               rename+reorder without colliding with that button. -->
          <button type="button" class="ls-tool-btn ls-move-btn" id="lsMoveBtn" aria-label="Administra tus listas: renombrar o reordenar">
            <span class="ls-btn-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 22 14" width="16" height="10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M2 7h18"/><path d="M6 3 2 7l4 4"/><path d="M16 3l4 4-4 4"/></svg></span>
            <span class="ls-btn-label">Administrar</span>
          </button>
          <!-- Dedicated delete mode for the lists region (Antonio's ruling,
               2026-07-29 polish): separate from the checklist's own Edit
               toggle, so deleting a whole list is never one accidental tap
               away from editing ticks. Now ALSO separate from Move (above),
               so it only ever does the one destructive thing. -->
          <button type="button" class="ls-tool-btn ls-del-btn" id="lsDelBtn" aria-label="Borrar una lista">
            <span class="ls-btn-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M2 8h12"/></svg></span>
            <span class="ls-btn-label">Borrar</span>
          </button>
        </div>
        <form class="ls-new-form" id="lsNewForm" hidden>
          <!-- Round 11 (Antonio): "Wedding" swapped out -- this site is
               built for club DJs, and a wedding-DJ suggestion here reads
               off-focus even though wedding DJs are welcome too. -->
          <input type="text" id="lsNewName" maxlength="40" placeholder="Festival, Club, Residencia, Otro..." aria-label="Ponle nombre a la lista nueva" aria-describedby="lsMsg" />
          <button type="submit" class="add-btn">Crear</button>
        </form>
        <p class="acct-msg" id="lsMsg" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- One toolbar, four short verbs (Antonio): Edit, Clear, Download PDF,
         Email. The old layout split them across two rows a full page apart,
         with "Download the printable PDF" and "Clear the list" spelling out
         what the short words already say. Edit and Email only make sense in
         Custom mode and hide outside it; Clear and Download work everywhere. -->
    <div class="custom-toolbar" id="customToolbar" hidden>
      <!-- Antonio's order: the two list actions, then the two ways to take the
           list with you. NOT "Email PDF": that button emails the list as text
           with a restore link, no PDF attached, and a label must never promise
           an attachment that does not arrive. EMAIL LIST is what it does. -->
      <button type="button" class="btn btn-neutral" id="editToggle"><span class="bl-full">Editar lista</span><span class="bl-short">Editar</span></button>
      <!-- "Reset list", not "Clear list" (Antonio): the promise is that the
           list goes back to how it was before you touched anything, which in
           Custom means your edits too, not just the ticks. That makes it
           destructive, so it asks once before it acts. -->
      <!-- BOTH states live in the button, stacked on one grid cell, and a
           class flips visibility: the button's width is the width of its
           widest state from the first paint, so arming can never grow it
           and reflow the toolbar (Antonio caught "Reset everything?"
           pushing Email list onto a second row). -->
      <button type="button" class="btn btn-neutral" id="resetBtn">
        <span class="rs-idle"><span class="bl-full">Restaurar lista</span><span class="bl-short">Restaurar</span></span>
        <!-- The armed press should feel like a command, not a repetition
             (Antonio, 2026-07-29). Two-tap safety unchanged; stacked-cell
             width trick unchanged (the idle state is the wider one, so
             nothing reflows). -->
        <span class="rs-arm" aria-hidden="true"><span class="bl-full">HAZLO</span><span class="bl-short">HAZLO</span></span>
      </button>
      <!-- Full words on desktop, one word on a phone: four buttons at full
           length wrap the toolbar onto a second line on mobile (Antonio). -->
      <!-- "Download list", not "Download PDF" (Antonio): the file is a PDF but
           the thing you are downloading is your list, same object as the other
           three buttons. -->
      <button type="button" class="btn btn-neutral" id="pdfBtn" data-event="checklist_download" data-placement="checklist_actions"><span class="bl-full">Descargar lista</span><span class="bl-short">Descargar</span></button>
      <!-- Email is LAST and darker in every mode (Antonio): it is the one
           action that requires registration, because an email needs an
           address, and the shade quietly says this one is different. -->
      <button type="button" class="btn btn-neutral btn-gated" id="backupBtn"><span class="bl-full">Enviar por correo</span><span class="bl-short">Correo</span></button>
      <!-- Round 9 (Antonio): the dismissible "Add notes, create sections..."
           tip used to live here as a standalone box with its own dismiss-X.
           It's redundant now that Edit list carries the identical copy as a
           press-and-hold/tap explainer (round 8), so it was removed rather
           than kept as a second, harder-to-dismiss copy of the same text. -->
      <form class="backup-form" id="backupForm" hidden>
        <input type="email" name="email" inputmode="email" autocomplete="email" placeholder="tu@email.com" aria-label="Tu correo" aria-describedby="backupMsg" />
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-neutral">Enviar</button>
      </form>
      <p class="backup-msg" id="backupMsg" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </div>

    {/* SHIPPED COLLAPSED, 2026-08-04. The script has always defaulted these to
            collapsed, but the MARKUP shipped them open, so every visit painted
            seven expanded groups and then collapsed them once a 58KB module had
            parsed. Measured cost: a single layout shift of 0.299, giving the page
            a CLS of 0.349 against a 0.1 budget. That is a Core Web Vitals
            failure on the site's most-used page, caused entirely by the initial
            state disagreeing with the intended one. Now they agree, and only a
            visitor who previously opened a group sees any movement at all.
            Measured after: 0.115. Print CSS forces these open regardless, so the
            printed sheet is unaffected. */}
    <div id="list" data-mode="basic" data-edit="off">
      {groups.map((g) => (
        <section class="panel task-group collapsed" data-group={g.id}>
          {/* h2, not h3 (2026-08-04): the page went h1 straight to h3 with no
              h2 anywhere, and these category panels ARE the top level under
              LEAVE HOME READY, all seven of them peers. Nothing moves: every
              size in this header comes from .group-title / .group-count /
              .group-ic, never from the heading element's own scale. */}
          <h2 class="group-h">
            <button type="button" class="group-toggle" data-toggle={g.id} aria-expanded="false" aria-controls={`body-${g.id}`}>
              <span class="group-ic" aria-hidden="true">
                {g.icon === 'disc' && <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="2.5"/></svg>}
                {g.icon === 'sliders' && <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M4 21v-7"/><path d="M4 10V3"/><path d="M12 21v-9"/><path d="M12 8V3"/><path d="M20 21v-5"/><path d="M20 12V3"/><path d="M2 14h4"/><path d="M10 8h4"/><path d="M18 16h4"/></svg>}
                {g.icon === 'star' && <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 2l3.09 6.26L22 9.27l-5 4.87 1.18 6.88L12 17.77l-6.18 3.25L7 14.14 2 9.27l6.91-1.01L12 2z"/></svg>}
                {g.icon === 'pin' && <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M20 10c0 6-8 12-8 12s-8-6-8-12a8 8 0 0 1 16 0z"/><circle cx="12" cy="10" r="3"/></svg>}
                {g.icon === 'sync' && <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 12a9 9 0 0 1 15-6.7L21 8"/><path d="M21 3v5h-5"/><path d="M21 12a9 9 0 0 1-15 6.7L3 16"/><path d="M3 21v-5h5"/></svg>}
                {g.icon === 'usb' && <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="9" y="2" width="6" height="7" rx="1"/><path d="M12 9v11"/><path d="M8 20h8"/></svg>}
                {g.icon === 'shield' && <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M20 13c0 5-3.5 7.5-7.66 8.95a1 1 0 0 1-.67-.01C7.5 20.5 4 18 4 13V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1c2 0 4.5-1.2 6.24-2.72a1 1 0 0 1 1.52 0C14.51 3.81 17 5 19 5a1 1 0 0 1 1 1z"/></svg>}
                {g.icon === 'plane' && <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M17.8 19.2 16 11l3.5-3.5C21 6 21.5 4 21 3c-1-.5-3 0-4.5 1.5L13 8 4.8 6.2c-.5-.1-.9.1-1.1.5l-.3.5c-.2.5-.1 1 .3 1.3L9 12l-2 3H4l-1 1 3 2 2 3 1-1v-3l3-2 3.5 5.3c.3.4.8.5 1.3.3l.5-.2c.4-.3.6-.7.5-1.2z"/></svg>}
              </span>
              <span class="group-title">{g.title}</span>
              <span class="group-meta">
                <span class="group-count" data-count={g.id}>0/0</span>
                <span class="chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              </span>
            </button>
            <!-- Custom + Edit: any category can go (Antonio). For built-in
                 groups this HIDES (Reset list brings them back); user
                 sections delete outright. -->
            <button type="button" class="group-remove" data-group-hide={g.id} aria-label={`Quitar la sección ${g.title}`}>&times;</button>
          </h2>
          <!-- Collapsed-state notes preview (Antonio): the briefing must be
               scannable WITHOUT expanding every section. Filled by JS from
               the saved notes, links clickable, click-through to edit. -->
          <div class="notes-preview" data-notes-prev={g.id}></div>
          <div class="group-body" id={`body-${g.id}`}>
            {g.blurbBase
              ? <p class="dim group-blurb"><span class="gb-base">{g.blurbBase}</span><span class="gb-adv">{g.blurb}</span></p>
              : <p class="dim group-blurb">{g.blurb}</p>}
            <ul class="tasks">
              {g.items.map((it) => (
                <li class="task" data-level={it.level} data-key={it.key}>
                  <label>
                    <input type="checkbox" data-key={it.key} data-level={it.level} />
                    <span class="box" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <span class="task-label">{it.label}</span>
                  </label>
                  <button type="button" class="task-remove" aria-label="Quitar" data-remove={it.key}>&times;</button>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
            <ul class="custom-tasks" data-custom={g.id}></ul>
            <div class="add-own">
              <input type="text" class="add-input" data-cat={g.id} placeholder="Agrega el tuyo..." aria-label={`Agregar un item tuyo a ${g.title}`} maxlength="80" />
              <button type="button" class="add-btn" data-add={g.id}>Agregar</button>
            </div>
            <!-- Category notes (Antonio's spec): one personal briefing area
                 per category, Custom mode only. Not a comments system: booth
                 config, contacts, set times, the gig-specific facts that are
                 not checklist items. Autosaves, prints, emails, restores. -->
            <div class="cat-notes">
              <button type="button" class="notes-toggle" data-notes-btn={g.id}>+ Agregar notas</button>
              <textarea class="notes-area" data-notes={g.id} rows="4" maxlength="2000" placeholder={`Notas de ${g.title}: setup, contactos, horarios, links, lo que este gig necesite.`} aria-label={`Tus notas de ${g.title}`} hidden></textarea>
              <span class="notes-hint" hidden>Toca fuera del campo para guardar.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
      ))}
    </div>

    <!-- Custom mode only: the DJ's own sections (Antonio's spec: "create
         sections"). New groups render above this row, built by JS with the
         same anatomy as the fixed ones. -->
    <div class="add-section" id="addSectionRow">
      <input type="text" class="add-input" id="addSectionInput" placeholder="Nombra una sección nueva..." aria-label="Nombra una sección nueva" maxlength="40" />
      <button type="button" class="add-btn" id="addSectionBtn">Crear sección</button>
    </div>

    <!-- The bottom actions row is gone: its two buttons moved into the toolbar
         above the list, where Antonio pointed out they belong. -->

    <!-- Round 4 (Antonio): the closing philosophy line used to sit here too
         (same sentence he'd already flagged as redundant up by the mode
         switch, coincidentally reused verbatim, it dates back to the
         original build, 2026-07-27). Removed for the same reason: doesn't
         earn its space on this page, reads as marketing copy. No inline
         margin-top override needed now that it isn't spacing the panel away
         from a paragraph that no longer exists, the panel falls back to
         the standard .panel-to-panel gap, closer to Logistics as asked. -->
    <div class="panel cta-exit">
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:0.9rem">¿Ya estás en el gig y algo falló?</h2>
      <a href="/es/emergency" class="btn btn-quiet-danger" data-event="begin_checkout" data-placement="checklist">Iniciar Emergency Mode</a>
      <p class="dim" style="font-size:0.86rem;margin:0.9rem 0 0">
        <a href="/es/card">Imprime la Emergency Card de una página</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    {/* Install recommendation, QUIET, at the foot. Preparing for a gig is the
        calm moment to set this up, which is why the page qualifies at all, but
        the reader came here to tick boxes: no accent rail, no icon disc, no
        filled button. Same words, less voice.
        variant="checklist" (2026-08-06) is what makes this card the new home of
        the two context links that used to sit under the h1: it adds the clause
        about the ticked list surviving a closed tab, and the quiet /prepare
        link. Only this page passes it. */}
    <InstallNudge lang="es" path="/checklist" tone="quiet" variant="checklist" placement="checklist_foot" />

    {/* The post-install registration ask, sharing the foot with the install
        card above it. Exactly one of the two can ever be visible: the card
        hides when the app is installed, this only shows when it is. */}
    <PostInstall lang="es" path="/checklist" />

    <!-- Icon-only circle, Antonio's EPK pattern: the site's boxes are square
         but its icon-only buttons are already circles (footer socials, the
         floating to-top), so this joins that family. Transparent background,
         arrow in white on hover. -->
    <div class="top-actions">
      <button type="button" class="top-circle" id="topBtn" aria-label="Volver arriba">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 19V5"/><path d="m5 12 7-7 7 7"/></svg>
      </button>
    </div>

    {/* PRINT ARTWORK, parked in the page so the download works OFFLINE
        (2026-08-04). The PDF is written into a blank window with
        document.write, so it has no origin and no stylesheet: a relative
        <img src> would not resolve and an absolute one would need signal,
        which is exactly what a DJ in a basement booth does not have. The
        service worker already caches this page, so shipping the artwork
        inside it means the printout is identical online and offline.
        Both are display:none and aria-hidden: they are data, not content. */}
    <template id="printLockup" set:html={lockupPrint} />
    <template id="printQr" set:html={printQr} />
  </main>
</Base>

<style is:global>
  .mode-switch {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 4px;
    padding: 4px;
    background: var(--surf);
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  /* Segment dividers (Antonio, round 4: this is where he actually wanted
     the hairline he first asked for on the Sync chip). align-self: stretch
     matches whatever height the row ends up, mobile or desktop. */
  .mode-switch-div { align-self: stretch; width: 1px; background: var(--edge); flex: 0 0 auto; }
  .mode-btn {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 0.55rem 1.2rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background var(--t-fast), color var(--t-fast);
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
  }
  .mode-btn.is-on { background: var(--text); color: #0a0a0b; }
  .mode-btn:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--green); outline-offset: 2px; }
  /* THE LOCK. inline-flex so the glyph sits on the label's own baseline row
     rather than in the text flow, where a 13px icon between two uppercase
     words would ride the descender line. The gap is the only width this state
     costs, which is why it is 0.32rem and not the 0.5rem used elsewhere: this
     row is measured to the pixel at 360 (see .mode-row's wrap rule below).
     Both the glyph and the screen-reader word are hidden, not removed, when
     the state clears, so unlocking is a class toggle and never DOM surgery on
     a control that has explainer listeners bound to it. */
  .mode-btn { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.32rem; }
  .mode-btn:not(.is-locked) .mode-lock,
  .mode-btn:not(.is-locked) .mode-lock-sr { display: none; }
  .mode-lock { flex: 0 0 auto; }
  /* It is sticky, so it is on screen the whole time you work the list. That
     makes height expensive: one line ("0 of 36 ready" + the percentage) with a
     thin bar under it, on every screen size. The READINESS label was saying
     what the numbers already say. */
  .readiness {
    background: var(--surf-2);
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 0.7rem 1.1rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
    position: sticky;
    top: 0.5rem;
    z-index: 5;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.15rem 1rem;
  }
  .readiness-top { display: contents; }
  .readiness-label { display: none; }
  .readiness-pct {
    grid-area: 1 / 2;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--text);
    transition: color 0.25s ease;
  }
  .readiness.is-ready .readiness-pct { color: var(--green); }
  .meter {
    grid-area: 2 / 1 / 2 / 3;
    height: 6px;
    background: var(--bg);
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
  }
  /* GREEN ONLY, settled (Antonio): a red->green gradient was built, seen,
     and reverted the same hour. His words: a red bar stresses an artist
     who packed everything for this one gig, "this is not a game". The
     meter encourages, it never judges. Do not reintroduce the gradient. */
  .meter-fill { height: 100%; width: 0%; background: var(--green); border-radius: var(--radius-pill); transition: width 0.3s ease; }
  .readiness-sub { grid-area: 1 / 1; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text-dim); }
  .readiness.is-ready .readiness-sub { color: var(--green); }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .readiness { padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem; gap: 0.1rem 0.8rem; }
    .readiness-pct { font-size: 1.35rem; }
    .meter { height: 5px; }
  }

  /* NO padding override, deliberately (2026-08-05). Mirror of the English
     checklist: padding: 0 never applied on the live site until the Aug 4
     perf pass flipped the CSS order, thinning every collapsed card by 40px.
     .panel's var(--sp-5) is the approved geometry. See the English file. */
  .task-group { overflow: hidden; }
  /* Round 11.5 follow-up (Antonio, live review): global.css's shared .panel
     class sets margin-bottom: 1.25rem, which every .task-group inherits (it
     also carries .panel) -- that's what set the box-to-box gap between
     Music/DJ Gear/etc, and it didn't match the 1.6rem rhythm the rest of
     this page's stack now uses (mode-row/readiness/list-switch/custom-
     toolbar). Overridden here, scoped to THIS page only (Astro scopes
     component styles automatically), rather than changing .panel itself in
     global.css, which would also move spacing on every other page that
     uses a plain .panel for something unrelated to this rhythm. This also
     evens out the gap before the emergency exit panel at the bottom of the
     list, since that gap is set by the LAST .task-group's own margin-bottom,
     not by .cta-exit's. */
  .task-group.panel { margin-bottom: 1.6rem; }
  /* EDIT MODE IS A VISIBLE STATE (Antonio). While editing: the toggle button
     goes green, and so does every group that is open, i.e. the ones whose
     items you can actually touch right now. Collapsed groups stay neutral,
     which quietly says "expand me and I become editable too". Leave edit mode
     and all of it reverts. One colour, one meaning: green marks where editing
     is live. */
  #editToggle.editing {
    border-color: var(--green); color: var(--green);
  }
  #list[data-edit="on"] .task-group:not(.collapsed) {
    border-color: var(--green);
  }

  /* Collapsible header is a full-width button living inside the h3. */
  .group-h { margin: 0; }
  .group-toggle {
    width: 100%;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* Icon, then title hugging it on the LEFT, count+chevron pushed right.
       (space-between briefly centered the title when the icon arrived.) */
    justify-content: flex-start;
    /* 0, not 0.15rem: .group-ic's own margin-right is the icon-to-title gap
       now (see --tg-gap above), and .group-title's flex: 1 keeps the count and
       chevron hard right, so this gap only ever affected the icon. */
    gap: 0;
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    text-align: left;
    padding: 1.1rem 1.5rem;
    font: inherit;
    color: inherit;
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
  }
  .group-title {
    flex: 1; text-align: left;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 3.6vw, 1.35rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .group-meta { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.85rem; flex: 0 0 auto; }
  .group-count {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--text-faint);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .group-count.done { color: var(--green); }
  .chev {
    width: 9px; height: 9px;
    border-right: 2px solid var(--text-dim);
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--text-dim);
    transform: rotate(45deg);           /* points down when expanded */
    margin-top: -3px;
    transition: transform var(--t-slow);
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  .group-toggle:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--green); outline-offset: -2px; }

  .group-body { padding: 0 1.5rem 1.1rem; }
  .task-group.collapsed .group-body { display: none; }
  .task-group.collapsed .chev { transform: rotate(-45deg); } /* points right when collapsed */

  .group-blurb { margin: 0 0 0.4rem; font-size: 0.92rem; }

  .tasks { list-style: none; margin: 0.4rem 0 0; padding: 0; }
  .task { border-bottom: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge-soft); display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .task:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .task > label { flex: 1; }
  /* A blurb can differ by mode (DJ Gear: Base has no laptop, so no "what
     can save your set"). Base shows gb-base, other modes show gb-adv. */
  #list[data-mode="basic"] .gb-adv { display: none; }
  #list:not([data-mode="basic"]) .gb-base { display: none; }
  /* Basic mode hides the advanced items so the mobile list stays short. */
  #list[data-mode="basic"] .task[data-level="advanced"] { display: none; }

  .task label {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    /* Same two tokens as the group header above, so the checkbox lands on the
       card inset (the 0.2rem of horizontal padding used to push it 3.2px off)
       and the label lands on the shared text column. */
    gap: var(--tg-gap);
    padding: 0.85rem 0;
    cursor: pointer;
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
  }
  /* [type=checkbox], not all inputs: the bare selector also caught the RENAME
     text input, crushing it to an invisible 1px sliver. That was Antonio's
     "edit deletes everything" bug: the input existed, had the text, took the
     keystrokes, and could not be seen. */
  .task input[type="checkbox"] { position: absolute; opacity: 0; width: 1px; height: 1px; }
  .box {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: var(--tg-mark);
    height: var(--tg-mark);
    margin-top: 1px;
    border: 1.5px solid var(--text-faint);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--bg);
    position: relative;
    transition: background var(--t-fast), border-color var(--t-fast);
  }
  .box::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 8px; top: 4px;
    width: 6px; height: 11px;
    border: solid #0a0a0b;
    border-width: 0 2.5px 2.5px 0;
    transform: rotate(45deg) scale(0);
    transition: transform var(--t-fast);
  }
  .task input:checked + .box { background: var(--green); border-color: var(--green); }
  .task input:checked + .box::after { transform: rotate(45deg) scale(1); }
  .task input:focus-visible + .box { outline: 2px solid var(--green); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .task-label { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--text); transition: color var(--t-fast); }
  .task input:checked ~ .task-label {
    color: var(--text-faint);
    text-decoration: line-through;
    text-decoration-color: var(--text-faint);
  }

  .checklist-actions { margin-top: 1.4rem; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.7rem; }
  .checklist-actions .btn { width: auto; display: inline-block; margin: 0; }

  /* Desktop: the storage promise starts its own line under the intro. */
  @media (min-width: 641px) { .intro-b { display: block; } }

  /* Same amber-edge "notice" language as the cookie banner and the explainer
     popovers (Antonio, earlier round: "the same as you have when you do the
     login... cookies warning") -- a DJ already recognises this visual
     vocabulary as "read this." Static and inline, not fixed/floating: it
     must never cover the toolbar or intercept a tap the way the cookie
     banner briefly can. */
  .backup-note {
    position: relative;
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem; padding: 12px 2.5rem 12px 16px;
    background: var(--surf); border: var(--stroke) solid var(--amber); border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .bn-t { margin: 0; font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--text-dim); }
  .bn-h {
    display: block; font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 800;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.02em; font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--amber); margin-bottom: 3px;
  }
  .bn-t a { color: var(--text); text-decoration: underline; }
  /* Dismiss-forever X (Antonio's follow-up ask): a real 44px-ish tap target
     in the corner, same "quiet until touched" language as the rest of the
     page -- gray at rest, brightens on hover/focus, never filled. */
  .bn-close {
    position: absolute; top: 6px; right: 6px;
    width: 32px; height: 32px; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    background: none; border: 0; color: var(--text-faint); font-size: 1.35rem; line-height: 1;
    cursor: pointer; padding: 0;
  }
  @media (hover: hover) { .bn-close:hover { color: var(--text); } }
  .bn-close:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--amber); outline-offset: 1px; }
  @media (min-width: 560px) { .bn-h { display: inline; margin-right: 6px; margin-bottom: 0; } }
  .top-actions { margin: 1.1rem 0 0; text-align: center; }
  /* Below the circle, the page ends. The gap down to the footer's top rule
     must MATCH the 1.1rem gap up to the emergency panel (Antonio measured
     it by eye on an iPhone: the default 4rem page bottom read as a hole).
     .wrap's own padding-bottom is the culprit, trimmed here page-locally;
     the footer's 1.3rem margin-top supplies the rest of the breathing. */
  main.wrap { padding-bottom: 0.4rem; }
  /* Antonio's EPK circle: transparent, thin edge, arrow brightens to white. */
  .top-circle {
    width: 46px; height: 46px; border-radius: var(--radius-round);
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    background: transparent; color: var(--text-dim);
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge); cursor: pointer;
    transition: color var(--t-fast), border-color var(--t-fast);
  }
  .top-circle:hover { color: var(--text); border-color: var(--text-dim); }

  .unlock {
    background: var(--surf-2); border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge); border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1.2rem 1.3rem; margin: 0 0 1.6rem;
  }
  .unlock[hidden] { display: none; }
  .unlock-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.05rem;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: -0.01em; margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
  }
  .unlock-copy { font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0 0 0.9rem; }
  /* Google-first layout (round 5): the Google button sits above the fold of
     this panel with nothing else competing with it; the toggle below reveals
     the form only for the DJs who need it. */
  .unlock-email-toggle {
    display: block; background: none; border: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: var(--text-dim); font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.85rem;
    text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; cursor: pointer;
  }
  .unlock-email-toggle[hidden] { display: none; }
  @media (hover: hover) { .unlock-email-toggle:hover { color: var(--text); } }
  .unlock-form { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; }
  .unlock-form[hidden] { display: none; }
  .bl-short { display: none; }
  .unlock-form input {
    width: 100%; padding: 0.9rem 1rem; font-family: inherit; font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--text); background: var(--bg); border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge-strong); border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .unlock-form input::placeholder { color: var(--text-faint); }
  .unlock-form input:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--green); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .unlock-form .btn { margin: 0; }
  .unlock-msg:empty { display: none; }
  .unlock-msg { margin: 0.8rem 0 0; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--text-dim); }
  .unlock-msg.ok { color: var(--green); }
  .unlock-msg.err { color: var(--red); }
  /* Register and the anti-spam note share a row on desktop (Antonio: the
     panel compacts, everything moves up); phones stack them again. */
  .unlock-actions { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.8rem; }
  @media (min-width: 520px) {
    .unlock-form { flex-direction: row; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .unlock-form input { flex: 1 1 220px; }
    .unlock-form .btn { width: auto; white-space: nowrap; }
    .unlock-actions {
      flex: 1 1 100%; flex-direction: row; align-items: center; gap: 1.2rem;
    }
    .unlock-actions .unlock-note { margin: 0; }
  }
  .unlock-note { margin: 0; font-size: 0.76rem; color: var(--text-faint); }
  .unlock-note a { color: var(--text-dim); }

  /* Custom mode: your own items. Editing (delete/add) is gated behind Edit. */
  .custom-tasks { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  #list:not([data-mode="custom"]) .custom-tasks { display: none; }
  #list[data-mode="custom"] .task.removed { display: none; }
  /* Custom items now live in the same list as the built-ins so the whole
     group can be reordered as one. Hidden outside Custom mode. */
  #list:not([data-mode="custom"]) .task-custom { display: none; }
  /* Drag handle: only in Custom + Edit. touch-action none so dragging the
     handle reorders instead of scrolling the page. */
  .task-drag {
    display: none; background: none; border: 0; color: var(--text-faint);
    line-height: 0; cursor: grab;
    /* 44x44 minimum, because you have to hit this exactly to start a drag and
       Antonio had to aim for it. The arrow inside is now 15x24, so the padding
       is what gets the target to a thumb-sized 44. */
    padding: 10px 14px;
    min-width: 44px; min-height: 44px;
    display: none; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    touch-action: none; flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  .task-drag:hover { color: var(--text-dim); }
  .task-drag:active { cursor: grabbing; color: var(--text); }
  /* The rename pencil. Same recipe as the drag handle: small quiet icon,
     thumb-sized target, visible only where renaming is actually possible. */
  .task-edit {
    display: none; background: none; border: 0; color: var(--text-faint);
    line-height: 0; cursor: pointer;
    padding: 10px; min-width: 40px; min-height: 40px;
    align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  .task-edit:hover { color: var(--green); }
  #list[data-mode="custom"][data-edit="on"] .task-edit { display: flex; }
  #list[data-mode="custom"][data-edit="on"] .task-drag { display: flex; }
  /* The row you are holding lifts off the list and follows your finger; the
     rows it displaces slide out of the way. Same on mobile and desktop, it is
     all pointer events. */
  .task.dragging {
    background: var(--surf-2);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-raised);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    border-bottom-color: transparent;
    position: relative;
    z-index: 6;
    cursor: grabbing;
  }
  .task.dragging .task-drag { color: var(--green); cursor: grabbing; }
  /* No text selection, no long-press callout, no blue highlight while you are
     editing. Putting a finger on a row used to select the words. */
  body.dragging-row { user-select: none; -webkit-user-select: none; }

  /* NOTHING in the list is selectable. The old rules only covered edit mode and
     only certain children, so a drag that started a few pixels off the handle
     ran a range selection straight down the list and painted several rows blue,
     including the remove buttons. A checklist is something you tick and drag,
     never something you highlight, so the whole list opts out. The rename input
     is the one exception and re-enables it below. */
  #list, #list .task, #list .task-label, #list .group-toggle, .task-drag {
    user-select: none; -webkit-user-select: none;
    -webkit-touch-callout: none; -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .task { transition: none !important; }
  }
  .task-label-edit {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0; font: inherit; color: var(--text);
    background: var(--surf-2); border: var(--stroke) solid var(--green);
    border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 0.2rem 0.45rem;
    /* The one place selection MUST work: you cannot rename what you cannot
       select. Overrides the blanket rule above. */
    user-select: text; -webkit-user-select: text;
  }
  .task-remove {
    flex: 0 0 auto; display: none; background: transparent; border: none; color: var(--text-faint);
    font-size: 1.4rem; line-height: 1; cursor: pointer; padding: 0 0.2rem 0 0.6rem;
  }
  /* Custom-scoped like the pencil and the arrows: a remove button in Basic
     mode (the iPhone bug) deletes from a list the mode cannot re-add to. */
  #list[data-mode="custom"][data-edit="on"] .task-remove { display: block; }
  .task-remove:hover { color: var(--red); }

  /* margin-bottom matched to .readiness / .mode-row (1.6rem, round 4): one
     consistent gap value from the mode switch down through the readiness
     bar to the first checklist box, instead of three different ones. */
  .custom-toolbar { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.8rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-bottom: 1.6rem; }
  .custom-toolbar[hidden] { display: none; }
  /* Transparent, not gray slabs (Antonio): the toolbar reads as quiet
     outlined actions on the page background, same language as the homepage
     search box. Two-selector specificity beats global .btn regardless of
     load order. All four buttons share Email list's behaviour, dim at rest,
     white on hover/touch (Antonio); click states like the armed red Reset
     keep their own colors via higher specificity. */
  .custom-toolbar .btn {
    width: auto; display: inline-block; margin: 0;
    background: transparent; color: var(--text-dim);
    transition: color var(--t-fast), border-color var(--t-fast);
  }
  .custom-toolbar .btn:hover, .custom-toolbar .btn:active {
    color: var(--text); border-color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  /* Armed reset: the button itself becomes the confirmation. Both labels
     occupy the same grid cell; visibility flips, width never moves. */
  #resetBtn { display: inline-grid; }
  #resetBtn > span { grid-area: 1 / 1; }
  #resetBtn .rs-arm { visibility: hidden; }
  #resetBtn.armed { border-color: var(--red); color: var(--red); }
  #resetBtn.armed .rs-idle { visibility: hidden; }
  #resetBtn.armed .rs-arm { visibility: visible; }
  /* The gated action: darker than its neighbours, which quietly says this one
     asks something of you. Registered users see it behave like any button. */
  /* .btn.btn-gated, two classes on purpose: global.css loads AFTER page CSS,
     so a single-class rule here loses the cascade to .btn's own background. */
  .btn.btn-gated { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text-dim); }
  .btn.btn-gated:hover { color: var(--text); }

  .add-own { display: none; gap: 8px; margin: 0.6rem 0 0.2rem; }
  #list[data-mode="custom"][data-edit="on"] .add-own { display: flex; }

  /* Category notes: Custom mode only, quiet by design. */
  .cat-notes { display: none; margin: 0.7rem 0 0.2rem; }
  #list[data-mode="custom"] .cat-notes { display: block; }
  .notes-toggle {
    background: none; border: 0; padding: 0.4rem 0; cursor: pointer;
    font-family: inherit; font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--text-faint);
  }
  .notes-toggle:hover { color: var(--text-dim); }
  .notes-toggle[hidden] { display: none; }
  .notes-area {
    width: 100%; resize: vertical; min-height: 84px;
    padding: 0.75rem 0.9rem; font-family: inherit; font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.5; color: var(--text);
    background: var(--bg); border: var(--stroke) dashed var(--edge-strong); border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .notes-area::placeholder { color: var(--text-faint); }
  .notes-area:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--green); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .notes-area[hidden] { display: none; }
  /* "Click outside to save.": there is no save button, say so while typing. */
  .notes-hint { display: block; margin-top: 0.3rem; font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--text-faint); }
  .notes-hint[hidden] { display: none; }

  /* Collapsed-state preview: only in Custom, only when the section is
     collapsed AND has notes (.has). Muted, wraps, line breaks kept. */
  .notes-preview { display: none; }
  #list[data-mode="custom"] .task-group.collapsed .notes-preview.has {
    display: block; padding: 0 1.5rem 0.95rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--text-faint);
    white-space: pre-wrap; word-break: break-word; cursor: pointer;
  }
  .notes-preview a { color: var(--text-dim); text-decoration: underline; }
  .notes-preview a:hover { color: var(--red); }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    #list[data-mode="custom"] .task-group.collapsed .notes-preview.has { padding: 0 1.2rem 0.85rem; }
  }

  /* Group header icon: same monochrome stroke language as everywhere. */
  /* ONE TEXT COLUMN, ONE MARK COLUMN (2026-08-06). Inside an expanded group,
     four different left edges were in play at 1280: the icon and the blurb at
     321, the checkbox at 324.2, the group title at 349.2 and the task label at
     361.8, so "Music" and the first task under it started 12.6px apart and
     nothing in the card lined up with anything else.
     The two rules that set those edges now read their geometry from the same
     two custom properties, so they cannot drift again: the icon sits in a box
     exactly as wide as a checkbox, and the same gap follows both. Result: the
     icon, the checkbox and the blurb all start on the card's inset, and the
     group title and every task label start on one text column 24px + gap
     inside it. The 17px icon is left-aligned in its 24px box, not centred, so
     its own left edge lands on the checkbox's. */
  #list { --tg-mark: 24px; --tg-gap: 0.85rem; }
  .group-ic {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: var(--tg-mark); margin-right: var(--tg-gap);
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  /* "Add section" row: Custom + Edit only, same anatomy as add-own. */
  .add-section { display: none; gap: 8px; margin: 1rem 0 0; }
  #list[data-mode="custom"][data-edit="on"] ~ .add-section { display: flex; }
  .add-section .add-input {
    flex: 1; padding: 0.75rem 0.9rem; font-family: inherit; font-size: 16px; color: var(--text);
    background: var(--bg); border: var(--stroke) dashed var(--edge-strong); border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .add-section .add-input::placeholder { color: var(--text-faint); }
  .add-section .add-input:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--green); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .add-section .add-btn {
    flex: 0 0 auto; background: var(--surf); border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge); border-radius: var(--radius);
    color: var(--text); font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.03em; padding: 0 1rem; cursor: pointer;
  }
  /* Section delete control, Custom + Edit only, sits RIGHT of the header
     (the h3 is a flex row so the x never wraps under the full-width
     toggle). Built-ins hide, user sections delete. */
  .group-h { display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .group-h .group-toggle { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }
  .group-remove {
    display: none; background: none; border: 0; color: var(--text-faint);
    font-size: 18px; line-height: 1; cursor: pointer;
    padding: 0 0.9rem; align-self: stretch; align-items: center;
  }
  .group-remove:hover { color: var(--red); }
  #list[data-mode="custom"][data-edit="on"] .group-remove { display: inline-flex; }
  /* A built-in category the DJ removed in Custom: gone in Custom only. */
  #list[data-mode="custom"] .task-group.user-hidden { display: none; }

  .backup-form { display: flex; gap: 8px; flex-basis: 100%; margin-top: 0.3rem; }
  .backup-form[hidden] { display: none; }
  .backup-form input {
    flex: 1; padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem; font-family: inherit; font-size: 16px; color: var(--text);
    background: var(--bg); border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge-strong); border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .backup-form input::placeholder { color: var(--text-faint); }
  .backup-form .btn { margin: 0; width: auto; }
  .backup-msg { flex-basis: 100%; margin: 0.4rem 0 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-dim); }
  .backup-msg:empty { display: none; }
  .backup-msg.ok { color: var(--green); }
  .backup-msg.err { color: var(--red); }
  .add-own .add-input {
    flex: 1; padding: 0.75rem 0.9rem; font-family: inherit; font-size: 16px; color: var(--text);
    background: var(--bg); border: var(--stroke) dashed var(--edge-strong); border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .add-own .add-input::placeholder { color: var(--text-faint); }
  .add-own .add-input:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--green); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .add-own .add-btn {
    flex: 0 0 auto; background: var(--surf); border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge); border-radius: var(--radius);
    color: var(--text); font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.03em; padding: 0 1rem; cursor: pointer;
  }
  @media (hover: hover) { .add-own .add-btn:hover { border-color: var(--text-dim); } }

  /* Mode switch + the sync chip share a row (Antonio's ruling, 2026-07-29
     polish: the chip lives beside Custom, not a full-width row under the
     meter). Dim grey and a verb ("Sync") until signed in, then a green
     state ("Synced"), gray never means "please press me" (his correction).
     Round 4: margin-bottom matched to the readiness meter's own
     margin-bottom (1.6rem) so the gap above and below the readiness bar
     reads as the same rhythm, now that the mode-tagline that used to sit
     here is gone. */
  .mode-row { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0.6rem; margin-bottom: 1.6rem; }
  .mode-row .mode-switch { margin-bottom: 0; }
  /* Groups the info toggle + Sync chip as one unit so .mode-row's
     space-between still reads as "modes on the left, status on the right",
     and so the pair wraps together on narrow phones instead of splitting. */
  .mode-row-right { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem; flex: 0 0 auto; }
  /* Rectangle, same shape language as every other button on the page
     (Antonio: the pill "looked strange" beside Reset/Download/Email). */
  .acct-chip {
    flex: 0 0 auto; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem;
    cursor: pointer; border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge); border-radius: var(--radius);
    /* Transparent, not filled (Antonio, round 5): no background in ANY
       state, idle, open or synced. Only the border and text color carry
       the state (gray idle, green synced), same "quiet until it matters"
       language as the rest of the page. */
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--text-faint); font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.7rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.85rem;
  }
  @media (hover: hover) { .acct-chip:hover { color: var(--text-dim); border-color: var(--text-dim); } }
  .acct-chip.is-synced { color: var(--green); border-color: var(--green); }
  /* Chevron now matches the category boxes (Music, DJ Gear), not the Lists
     header: right when closed, down when open (Antonio's ask, round 3). The
     shape is the same corner .ls-chev already draws, just rotated the other
     way at each state. */
  .acct-chip .ls-chev { transform: rotate(-45deg); margin-top: -2px; }
  .acct-chip[aria-expanded="true"] .ls-chev { transform: rotate(45deg); margin-top: -3px; }
  /* Desktop sizing (Antonio, round 3): the chip and the (i) button should
     stand the same height as the mode-switch box beside them, width sized to
     their own content rather than stretched. Mobile keeps its own compact
     padding below, untouched. */
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .acct-chip, .explain-btn { height: 2.6rem; }
    .explain-btn { width: 2.6rem; padding: 0; }
    /* 10% bigger glyph (Antonio, live desktop review), then another +20%
       on top of that same round (0.99rem -> 1.19rem) -- same "box stays
       put, glyph grows" split already used for the mobile version below
       (round 11's "twice that size" doubling). Box unchanged at 2.6rem.
       Compound selector, not a plain .explain-btn override: the same
       cascade-order trap documented at .mode-row-right .explain-btn below
       applies here too -- the base unconditional .explain-btn rule sits
       AFTER this media query in source order, so equal specificity would
       silently lose to it. */
    .mode-row-right .explain-btn { font-size: 1.19rem; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    /* Round 4 (Antonio): on mobile the (i) + Sync pair belongs on the SAME
       line as Base/Advanced/Custom, same as desktop, not wrapped to a row
       of its own. No more flex-wrap here; instead the mode buttons and the
       chip/info pair are all tightened so the whole row fits from 360px up
       (measured with Playwright, not eyeballed, see build notes). */
    .mode-btn { padding: 0.5rem 0.45rem; font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.02em; }
    .mode-switch { gap: 2px; padding: 3px; }
    /* THE RIGHT CLUSTER WRAPS WHEN IT STOPS FITTING, IT DOES NOT CLIP
       (2026-08-06). Round 4 (Antonio) removed flex-wrap from this row so the
       (i) + Sync pair stayed on the same line as Base/Advanced/Custom, and
       tightened the type until the row fitted at 360px. Measured on 2026-08-05
       it no longer did: .mode-row's right edge is 340 at a 360px viewport,
       matching the h1, the advice panel, the readiness bar and the button row,
       but .mode-row-right reached 349.4 in English and 363.9 in Spanish, so the
       Spanish SYNC chip sat 3.9px outside the viewport and was visibly cut.
       Tightening the type further was the wrong lever: it was already at
       0.72rem, and the mode buttons have since gained a lock glyph, which
       costs width the row does not have at 390 either.
       So flex-wrap comes back, and Antonio's ruling still holds wherever it
       physically can: BOTH children are flex: 0 0 auto, so the cluster stays on
       line one at every width where it fits and drops to its own row, still
       right-aligned (margin-left: auto, since space-between cannot align a lone
       wrapped item), only when it does not. No breakpoint is guessed: the
       browser wraps at the real threshold, which is what a hardcoded 375px was
       approximating. Verified zero horizontal overflow at 360/390/1280 in all
       three languages. */
    .mode-row { gap: 0.45rem 0.3rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .mode-switch { flex: 0 0 auto; }
    .mode-row-right { margin-left: auto; }
    /* Round 5, third pass (Antonio, mobile screenshot): Sync + (i) read
       visibly smaller than Base/Advanced/Custom, grown to match, same
       recipe as the desktop `height: 2.6rem` match above, tuned by
       measurement for this breakpoint's own padding/font-size.
       UPDATED ROUND 11 (Antonio, mobile screenshot again): still not a
       true match -- the chip's font read smaller than the mode buttons'
       and the (i) button's read LARGER, and padding-derived sizing left
       the (i) box taller than it was wide (a portrait rectangle, his
       exact complaint). The desktop `@media (hover: hover)` branch above
       already forces the chip AND the (i) button to an identical
       EXPLICIT height (and squares the (i) button by also setting its
       width to that same value) -- nothing mirrored that fix for touch
       devices, so mobile only ever got padding-derived sizing, which
       can't guarantee equal height let alone a square. Same fix, mobile
       numbers: explicit height on both, explicit matching width on the
       (i) button only (the chip's width still needs to fit its own
       variable-length text), font-size matched to `.mode-btn` on both. */
    .acct-chip { font-size: 0.72rem; height: 2.3rem; padding: 0 0.6rem; gap: 0.3rem; }
    /* Compound selector, not just .explain-btn (same cascade-order trap as
       .acct-del, round 5): the base .explain-btn rule further down this
       file is unconditional and sits AFTER this media query in source
       order, so a plain .explain-btn override here silently lost every
       tie. .mode-row-right .explain-btn is more specific and wins
       regardless of where the base rule sits. */
    /* UPDATED ROUND 11, second pass (Antonio, live again): matching the
       (i) glyph's font-size to .mode-btn's word-sized text left the
       actual SYMBOL reading tiny -- a single circled-letter glyph doesn't
       carry the same visual weight as a full word at the same nominal
       font-size. Antonio's ask was explicit: "twice that size." Doubled
       the glyph's font-size only; the box itself stays at the same
       height/width as the Sync chip so the row still lines up exactly as
       he asked for two messages ago -- there's easily enough headroom (a
       ~23px glyph inside a ~37px square, centered by the existing flex
       rule) for this to just read bigger, not clip or unbalance the box.
       ROUND 11.5 follow-up: now too big the other way -- reduced 12%
       (1.44rem -> 1.27rem). Box stays put, same reasoning as before. */
    .mode-row-right .explain-btn { font-size: 1.27rem; height: 2.3rem; width: 2.3rem; padding: 0; }
    .mode-row-right { gap: 0.3rem; }
  }
  .acct-card {
    margin-bottom: 1rem; padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
    /* Green border (Antonio, round 5): the only trigger that opens this
       card is the Sync/Synced chip, so a green outline ties the two
       together visually the instant it opens, whether signed in or not. */
    background: var(--surf); border: var(--stroke) solid var(--green); border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .acct-card[hidden] { display: none; }
  /* Borrows .unlock-title's type wholesale (the gate panel above uses it for
     exactly this job) and only adds the gap under it, because this heading
     sits directly on .acct-copy rather than on .unlock-copy's own margin. */
  .acct-gate-h { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  .acct-copy { font-size: 0.88rem; margin-bottom: 0.85rem; }
  .acct-who { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
  .gsi-holder { margin-bottom: 0.85rem; }
  .gsi-holder:empty { display: none; }
  .acct-form { display: flex; gap: 8px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  /* Explicit, not left to the UA default (same cascade-specificity trap
     documented on .acct-del in round 5): .acct-form sets its own display,
     tying [hidden] at equal specificity, decided by source order alone. */
  .acct-form[hidden] { display: none; }
  /* font-size must be 16px, not smaller (round 11.5 follow-up, real bug
     found investigating Antonio's live report): iOS Safari auto-zooms the
     whole page in when a text input's font-size is under 16px on focus.
     This one (0.9rem =~ 15.3px) was almost certainly why a second tester's
     phone "felt loose" right after being asked for her artist name here --
     the zoom triggers on focus and never resets, so the page reads as
     pannable/swipeable afterward. .unlock-form/.add-section/.backup-form
     inputs elsewhere on this page already got this right; this one and
     .ls-new-form/.ls-name-edit below didn't. */
  .acct-form input {
    flex: 1 1 200px; min-width: 0; background: var(--bg); color: var(--text);
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge-strong); border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 0.7rem 0.8rem; font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 16px;
  }
  .acct-form input::placeholder { color: var(--text-faint); }
  .acct-form input:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--green); outline-offset: 2px; }
  /* Sign out and Delete share one row (Antonio: smaller box). */
  .acct-actions { display: flex; gap: 8px; }
  .acct-actions .btn { flex: 1 1 0; min-width: 0; white-space: nowrap; text-align: center; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .acct-actions .btn { padding: 0.7rem 0.3rem; font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.01em; }
    /* Antonio flagged dead space around the two buttons on mobile: a
       tighter card inset and a smaller gap let them read edge-to-edge. */
    .acct-card { padding: 1rem 0.85rem; }
    .acct-actions { gap: 6px; }
  }
  .acct-profile { margin-bottom: 0.9rem; }
  .acct-profile input { flex: 1 1 100%; }
  .acct-profile[hidden] { display: none; }
  .unlock-alt { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.8rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: 0.9rem; }
  .unlock-alt[hidden] { display: none; }
  .gsi-wrap { position: relative; display: inline-block; }
  /* Compound selector, not just .acct-del (Antonio, round 5 bug): this was
     losing the cascade to global.css's .btn { display: block }, same
     specificity but .btn loads later, so the stacked-cell trick silently
     never applied. Both spans rendered as sequential inline text instead ,
     .rs-arm stayed invisible but still reserved its width right after
     .rs-idle, so the centered text-align pushed the VISIBLE text off to one
     side (left when idle, right when armed) instead of truly centering it.
     That's the "left-justified, then jumps right on click" bug. */
  .acct-actions .acct-del { display: inline-grid; }
  .acct-del > span { grid-area: 1 / 1; }
  .acct-del .rs-arm { visibility: hidden; }
  .acct-del.armed { border-color: var(--red); color: var(--red); }
  .acct-del.armed .rs-idle { visibility: hidden; }
  .acct-del.armed .rs-arm { visibility: visible; }
  /* Round 7: Sign out gets the same two-tap arm as Delete account. Same
     compound-selector lesson applied proactively this time, not after the
     fact: .acct-actions .acct-signout, not just .acct-signout, so this
     never loses the cascade tie to global.css's .btn { display: block }
     the way .acct-del did in round 5. */
  .acct-actions .acct-signout { display: inline-grid; }
  .acct-signout > span { grid-area: 1 / 1; }
  .acct-signout .rs-arm { visibility: hidden; }
  .acct-signout.armed { border-color: var(--red); color: var(--red); }
  .acct-signout.armed .rs-idle { visibility: hidden; }
  .acct-signout.armed .rs-arm { visibility: visible; }
  /* Round 7: was reading left-aligned against the centered buttons above it
     (Antonio). */
  .acct-fine { font-size: 0.78rem; margin-top: 0.7rem; text-align: center; }
  .acct-fine.err { color: var(--red); }
  .acct-msg { font-size: 0.82rem; margin-top: 0.55rem; min-height: 0; color: var(--text-dim); }
  .acct-msg.ok { color: var(--green); }
  .acct-msg.err { color: var(--red); }
  .acct-msg:empty { display: none; }

  /* Named-list switcher (Custom + signed in). One collapsed header row that
     names the active list; pills unfold below it. House-monochrome; the
     active list is simply the lit one. */
  /* Round 11.5 follow-up (Antonio, live review): was 1rem, smaller than the
     established 1.6rem rhythm .mode-row/.readiness/.custom-toolbar already
     share (round 4) -- made the gap ABOVE this box (readiness's own 1.6rem
     margin-bottom) read as bigger than the gap below it, when he wants
     every gap in this stack the same. Matched to 1.6rem. */
  .list-switch { margin-bottom: 1.6rem; }
  /* Author display beats the UA's [hidden] rule (same lesson as the toolbar
     buttons on Antonio's iPhone): state it explicitly. */
  .list-switch[hidden] { display: none; }
  .ls-head {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.55rem; width: 100%;
    background: none; border: none; padding: 0.15rem 0; cursor: pointer; text-align: left;
  }
  .list-switch .ls-label {
    color: var(--text-faint); font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }
  .ls-active { color: var(--text-dim); font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.85rem; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap; }
  @media (hover: hover) { .ls-head:hover .ls-active { color: var(--text); } }
  /* Round 11.5 follow-up (Antonio, live review): this chevron was backwards
     -- pointing DOWN while collapsed and UP while expanded, the opposite of
     every other accordion on the page (.chev on the category boxes: right
     when collapsed, down when expanded -- same convention the Sync chip's
     own .acct-chip .ls-chev override already followed since round 3). Base
     rule now matches that convention: right (-45deg) when closed, down
     (45deg) when open. Only affects #lsHead's chevron -- .acct-chip .ls-chev
     below has its own more specific override and was already correct. */
  .ls-chev {
    width: 7px; height: 7px; flex: 0 0 auto;
    border-right: 1.5px solid var(--text-faint); border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--text-faint);
    transform: rotate(-45deg); transition: transform var(--t-fast); margin-top: -2px;
  }
  .ls-head[aria-expanded="true"] .ls-chev { transform: rotate(45deg); margin-top: -3px; }
  .ls-body { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 0.7rem; }
  .ls-body[hidden] { display: none; }
  /* Wraps on desktop (plenty of width); on a phone this becomes a single
     horizontal-scroll row instead (Antonio's iPhone Pro Max complaint: too
     many pills wrapping ate the whole screen). See the max-width query
     further down for the touch-only override. */
  .ls-pills { display: flex; gap: 6px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .ls-pill {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px;
    background: none; border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge); border-radius: var(--radius);
    color: var(--text-dim); font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.82rem;
    padding: 0.42rem 0.7rem; cursor: pointer; max-width: 190px;
  }
  .ls-pill .ls-name { overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap; }
  @media (hover: hover) { .ls-pill:hover { border-color: var(--text-dim); color: var(--text); } }
  .ls-pill.is-on { border-color: var(--text); color: var(--text); }
  /* Delete mode (Antonio's ruling, 2026-07-29 polish, split further in round
     11.5): its own toggle, separate from Move below. While on, every pill
     but the active one shows a small x in its upper corner; same two-tap
     armed contract as everything else destructive here (Reset, Delete
     account). */
  /* Round 11.5 follow-up (real-tester report, Maya on iPhone 15 Pro): "the x
     to choose the list to delete was so small. She could not see it." Was
     17px with a 0.72rem (~12px) glyph -- bumped to 24px / 0.85rem, closer to
     (if still short of) the 44px minimum tap target used elsewhere on this
     page. Kept as a corner badge rather than moved inline, but the bigger
     circle needs more breathing room from the next pill in the row than the
     calm-state 6px gap gives it -- see .list-switch.deleting .ls-pills
     below, which only widens that gap while Delete mode is actually on. */
  .ls-x {
    display: none; position: absolute; top: -9px; right: -9px;
    width: 24px; height: 24px; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge); border-radius: var(--radius-round); background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--text-faint); font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1; padding: 0; cursor: pointer;
  }
  .list-switch.deleting .ls-x { display: inline-flex; }
  .list-switch.deleting .ls-pills { gap: 16px 10px; }
  .ls-pill.is-on .ls-x { display: none; }
  .ls-x.armed { color: var(--red); border-color: var(--red); }
  /* Round 9, split into its own "Move" mode in round 11.5 (Antonio: "why is
     the delete button giving me the option to move? it's a little bit
     strange"). Sideways version of the checklist's task-drag -- pills sit in
     a row, so you drag left/right, not up/down. */
  .ls-drag {
    display: none; background: none; border: 0; color: var(--text-faint);
    line-height: 0; cursor: grab; padding: 8px;
    min-width: 36px; min-height: 36px;
    align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    touch-action: none; flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  .ls-drag:hover { color: var(--text-dim); }
  .ls-drag:active { cursor: grabbing; color: var(--text); }
  .list-switch.moving .ls-drag { display: inline-flex; }
  .ls-pill.dragging {
    background: var(--surf-2);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-raised);
    position: relative;
    z-index: 6;
    cursor: grabbing;
  }
  .ls-pill.dragging .ls-drag { color: var(--green); cursor: grabbing; }
  /* Round 11.5: real list rename, not just checklist-item rename. Lives in
     the same Move mode as the drag handle -- Move is "organise your lists"
     (reorder + rename), Delete is the one purely destructive toggle. Same
     pencil glyph and click-to-edit-inline flow as the checklist item pencil
     (makeEditButton/beginRename), just pointed at a list name instead of a
     task label. */
  .ls-pencil {
    display: none; background: none; border: 0; color: var(--text-faint);
    line-height: 0; cursor: pointer; padding: 8px;
    min-width: 32px; min-height: 32px;
    align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  .ls-pencil:hover { color: var(--green); }
  .list-switch.moving .ls-pencil { display: inline-flex; }
  /* 16px, not smaller -- same iOS auto-zoom-on-focus fix as .acct-form/
     .ls-new-form inputs above. Widened slightly (was max-width: 150px) so
     16px text isn't cramped inside the pill. */
  .ls-name-edit {
    flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 60px; max-width: 170px; background: var(--bg); color: var(--text);
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--green); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 0.2rem 0.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 16px;
  }
  /* Three single-purpose icon+label buttons (New / Move / Delete), replacing
     the old two-button row where Delete also revealed reorder. Icon shows
     always; the word label is a desktop-only enhancement (below), so mobile
     gets a tight row of three small icon buttons instead of three full-word
     buttons wrapping onto their own line (Antonio's mobile space complaint). */
  .ls-toolbar { display: flex; gap: 6px; }
  .ls-tool-btn {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    background: none; border: var(--stroke) dashed var(--edge); border-radius: var(--radius);
    color: var(--text-faint); font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.82rem;
    padding: 0.42rem 0.6rem; cursor: pointer;
  }
  @media (hover: hover) { .ls-tool-btn:hover { color: var(--text); border-color: var(--text-dim); } }
  .ls-btn-ic { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; }
  /* Hidden by default (mobile default): the icon alone reads clearly enough
     at this size, and three full words wrap the toolbar. Desktop, which has
     the width and uses a mouse rather than a thumb, gets the word back. */
  .ls-btn-label { display: none; }
  @media (hover: hover) { .ls-btn-label { display: inline; margin-left: 0.35rem; } }
  .ls-move-btn.active { color: var(--text); border-color: var(--text); border-style: solid; }
  .ls-del-btn.active { color: var(--red); border-color: var(--red); border-style: solid; }
  .ls-new-form { display: flex; gap: 6px; flex: 1 1 100%; }
  .ls-new-form[hidden] { display: none; }
  .ls-new-form .add-btn {
    flex: 0 0 auto; background: var(--surf); border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge); border-radius: var(--radius);
    color: var(--text); font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.03em; padding: 0 1rem; cursor: pointer;
  }
  @media (hover: hover) { .ls-new-form .add-btn:hover { border-color: var(--text-dim); } }
  /* 16px, not smaller -- same iOS auto-zoom-on-focus fix as .acct-form
     input above (this is the "creating a list breaks the page/zooms"
     input Antonio reported directly). */
  .ls-new-form input {
    flex: 1 1 160px; min-width: 0; background: var(--bg); color: var(--text);
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge-strong); border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 0.55rem 0.7rem; font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 16px;
  }
  .ls-new-form input::placeholder { color: var(--text-faint); }
  .ls-new-form input:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--green); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .list-switch .acct-msg { flex: 1 1 100%; margin-top: 0; }
  /* Phone (Antonio, iPhone Pro Max screenshot): a wrapping pill row grew
     tall enough to push the toolbar off screen once he had more than a
     couple of lists. One horizontal-scroll line instead -- pills never
     shrink or wrap, the row just scrolls, same idea as a tab strip. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .ls-pills {
      flex-wrap: nowrap; overflow-x: auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
      scrollbar-width: none; padding-bottom: 2px;
    }
    .ls-pills::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }
    .ls-pill { flex: 0 0 auto; max-width: 150px; }
  }

  /* Info toggle + every per-control popover share this look, the same amber
     edge the cookie banner uses, so the whole thing reads as one system a
     DJ has already seen rather than something new to learn. The toggle
     itself is gray until switched on (Antonio, round 2): amber on this
     button specifically means "I am currently on", not just "here I am". */
  .explain-btn {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; cursor: pointer;
    /* Transparent (Antonio, round 5): matches the Sync chip, no fill in any
       state, amber lives only in the border and glyph when it's on. */
    background: transparent; border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge); border-radius: var(--radius);
    color: var(--text-faint); font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.7rem;
  }
  .explain-btn.mode-active { border-color: var(--amber); color: var(--amber); }
  .explain-btn[hidden] { display: none; }
  /* Fixed-position, JS-placed near whatever was pressed or hovered. One at a
     time, same as rekordbox: opening a new one always closes the last. */
  .explain-pop {
    position: fixed; z-index: 200; max-width: 280px;
    background: var(--surf-2); border: var(--stroke) solid var(--amber); border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 10px 14px; font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.84rem; line-height: 1.48;
    color: var(--text);
    box-shadow: 0 10px 44px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.66), 0 0 0 1px rgba(217, 164, 65, 0.18), 0 0 26px rgba(217, 164, 65, 0.14);
    animation: explainIn var(--t-fast)-out both;
  }
  @keyframes explainIn { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(6px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); } }
  /* Applied by JS to every covered control: holding it must never select
     its text or pop the phone's native long-press menu instead of ours. */
  .explain-target {
    -webkit-touch-callout: none; -webkit-user-select: none; user-select: none;
    touch-action: manipulation;
  }
  /* Round 8 (Antonio): clicking the (i) should show which controls have
     extra instructions all at once, not one at a time as each is
     hovered/tapped. Placed AFTER .acct-chip.is-synced and .mode-btn.is-on
     on purpose -- both tie this at the same (0,2,0) specificity, and this
     deliberately wins that tie so the green "Synced" look and the white
     "selected mode" fill both give way to the same amber wash while info
     mode is active. Pure class toggle, nothing else about those elements'
     state changes, so everything reverts exactly the moment info mode
     turns off. */
  .explain-target.info-lit { border: var(--stroke) solid var(--amber); background: var(--amber-dim); }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .meter-fill, .box, .box::after, .readiness-pct, .mode-btn, .chev, .ls-chev { transition: none; }
    .explain-pop { animation: none; }
  }

  /* Mobile width and alignment (kept last so it wins the cascade).
     The generic .panel padding was stacking on top of the card's own inset,
     so the checkboxes sat 21px right of the Done button and 40px of the card
     was dead space on the right. Zero the panel padding and let the header and
     the body share ONE inset, matched by the toolbar buttons, so the group
     title, the checkboxes and Done all start on the same vertical line.

     REBUILT 2026-08-03, after the journey sweep and Antonio's go. The page
     used to buy its edge-to-edge card by narrowing its OWN gutter to 0.75rem
     against the sitewide 1.25rem. That made /checklist the one page in 107
     whose frame did not line up: a 369px column against 353px everywhere
     else, with its left edge 8px further out than every other page.

     The card now bleeds past a STANDARD gutter instead of moving the page.
     A negative margin of exactly --site-pad puts the card edge on the
     viewport edge, and an inset of --site-pad inside it puts the group title,
     the checkboxes and Done on 1.25rem, the same vertical line as the page
     heading, the toolbar, and every other page on the site. Same card, frame
     restored, and the item text gains width because content now sits at
     1.25rem instead of 0.75 + 1.2 = 1.95rem. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    /* NO FULL BLEED (Antonio, 2026-08-05, from a live phone screenshot):
       the category cards must not be wider than the readiness bar above
       them. The negative --site-pad margins that pushed the card edges to
       the viewport edge are gone; the card now sits inside the same
       gutters as every other panel on the page. The toggle keeps its
       --site-pad inset, so the title lands at the same inner line as the
       readiness text. */
    .task-group.panel {
      padding-left: 0;
      padding-right: 0;
    }
    .group-toggle { padding: 0.9rem var(--site-pad); }
    .group-body { padding: 0 var(--site-pad) 0.95rem; }
    .task > label { padding-left: 0; padding-right: 0; }
    /* Four buttons, ONE row, on a 360px Samsung (Antonio: "breaking the line,
